solution:
Proof.  For each element \(x\in A\), define its “replication number”
\[
r_x \;=\; \#\bigl\{\,i : x\in A_i\bigr\}.
\]
We first show that
\[
\sum_{x\in A} r_x \;=\; 11\cdot 45
\quad\text{and}\quad
\sum_{x\in A} \binom{r_x}{2} \;=\;\binom{11}{2}\,9,
\]
and then deduce the desired bound on \(\bigl|\bigcup_iA_i\bigr|\).

1.  Since each \(A_i\) has size 45,
\[
\sum_{x\in A} r_x
\;=\;
\sum_{x\in A}\sum_{i=1}^{11} \mathbf{1}_{\{x\in A_i\}}
\;=\;
\sum_{i=1}^{11}\sum_{x\in A} \mathbf{1}_{\{x\in A_i\}}
\;=\;
\sum_{i=1}^{11} |A_i|
\;=\;
11\cdot 45 \;=\; 495.
\]

2.  Likewise, for the number of pairs \((i,j)\) with \(i<j\) that contain \(x\),
\[
\sum_{x\in A} \binom{r_x}{2}
\;=\;
\sum_{x\in A}\sum_{1\le i<j\le 11}
   \mathbf{1}_{\{x\in A_i\cap A_j\}}
\;=\;
\sum_{1\le i<j\le 11}\sum_{x\in A}
   \mathbf{1}_{\{x\in A_i\cap A_j\}}
\;=\;
\sum_{1\le i<j\le 11}\bigl|A_i\cap A_j\bigr|
\;=\;
\binom{11}{2}\,9
\;=\;
55\cdot 9
\;=\;
495.
\]

3.  From
\(\binom{r_x}{2}=\tfrac12(r_x^2-r_x)\) we get
\[
\sum_{x\in A} \bigl(r_x^2-r_x\bigr)
\;=\;
2\sum_{x\in A}\binom{r_x}{2}
\;=\;
2\cdot 495
\;=\;
990.
\]
Hence
\[
\sum_{x\in A} r_x^2
\;=\;
990 \;+\;\sum_{x\in A} r_x
\;=\;
990 \;+\;495
\;=\;
1485.
\]

4.  Let
\[
U
\;=\;
A_1\cup A_2\cup\cdots\cup A_{11},
\]
and write \(N=|U|\).  On \(U\) we have \(r_x\ge1\), while on \(A\setminus U\) we have \(r_x=0\).  Therefore
\[
\sum_{x\in A}r_x
\;=\;
\sum_{x\in U}r_x
\quad\text{and}\quad
\sum_{x\in A}r_x^2
\;=\;
\sum_{x\in U}r_x^2.
\]
By the Cauchy–Schwarz inequality,
\[
\Bigl(\sum_{x\in U}r_x\Bigr)^2
\;\le\;
\Bigl(\sum_{x\in U}1\Bigr)\,\Bigl(\sum_{x\in U}r_x^2\Bigr)
\;=\;
N\;\bigl(1485\bigr).
\]
But \(\sum_{x\in U}r_x=495\).  Hence
\[
495^2
\;\le\;
1485\,N
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
N\;\ge\;\frac{495^2}{1485}
\;=\;\frac{495}{3}
\;=\;165.
\]
Thus
\[
\bigl|A_1\cup\cdots\cup A_{11}\bigr|
\;=\;
N
\;\ge\;
165,
\]
as claimed.

Remark on equality.  Equality in Cauchy–Schwarz forces \(r_x\) to be constant (and nonzero) on \(U\).  Since \(\sum_{x\in U}r_x=495\) and \(|U|=165\), one must have \(r_x\equiv 3\) for all \(x\in U\).  In particular each element of \(U\) lies in exactly three of the \(A_i\), and each pair \(A_i,A_j\) intersects in
\[
\sum_{x\in U}\mathbf{1}_{\{x\in A_i\cap A_j\}}
\;=\;
\sum_{x\in U}\binom{r_x}{2}\Big/\binom{3}{2}
\;=\;
\frac{495}{55}
\;=\;
9.
\]

Example achieving equality.  Let
\[
U
\;=\;
\bigl\{\{i,j,k\}:1\le i<j<k\le 11\bigr\},
\]
so \(|U|=\binom{11}{3}=165\).  Adjoin to \(U\) any disjoint set \(D\) of 60 “dummy’’ points, and set
\[
A \;=\; U\;\cup\; D
\;\quad(\text{so }|A|=225).
\]
For each \(1\le i\le11\), define
\[
A_i
\;=\;
\bigl\{\,X\in U : i\in X\bigr\}.
\]
Then \(|A_i|=\binom{10}{2}=45\), and for \(i<j\),
\[
A_i\cap A_j
\;=\;
\bigl\{\,X\in U : \{i,j\}\subset X\bigr\}
\;
\Longrightarrow
\;
|A_i\cap A_j|
=\binom{9}{1}=9.
\]
Moreover \(\bigcup_{i=1}^{11}A_i=U\) has size 165, so this construction shows the bound is sharp.  ■

problem:
Prove that the system of equations

$$
\begin{aligned}
x^{6}+x^{3}+x^{3} y+y & =147^{157} \\
x^{3}+x^{3} y+y^{2}+y+z^{9} & =157^{147}
\end{aligned}
$$

has no integer solutions.

Prove that the system of equations

$$
\begin{aligned}
x^{6}+x^{3}+x^{3} y+y & =147^{157} \\
x^{3}+x^{3} y+y^{2}+y+z^{9} & =157^{147}
\end{aligned}
$$

has no integer solutions.

Prove that the system of equations

$$
\begin{aligned}
x^{6}+x^{3}+x^{3} y+y & =147^{157} \\
x^{3}+x^{3} y+y^{2}+y+z^{9} & =157^{147}
\end{aligned}
$$

has no integer solutions.

Prove that the system of equations

$$
\begin{aligned}
x^{6}+x^{3}+x^{3} y+y & =147^{157} \\
x^{3}+x^{3} y+y^{2}+y+z^{9} & =157^{147}
\end{aligned}
$$

has no integer solutions.

solution:
Proof.  Denote for each row \(i\) and column \(j\) of the positive matrix \(\bigl(a_{ij}\bigr)_{1\le i\le m,\;1\le j\le n}\) the “row–harmonic mean” and “column–harmonic mean” by

\[
R_i\;:=\;\Bigl(\sum_{j=1}^n\frac1{a_{ij}}\Bigr)^{-1}, 
\qquad
C_j\;:=\;\Bigl(\sum_{i=1}^m a_{ij}\Bigr)^{-1}.
\]
Then the inequality to prove is
\[
\sum_{i=1}^m R_i 
\;\le\;
\Bigl(\sum_{j=1}^n C_j\Bigr)^{-1}.
\tag{★}
\]
Observe first that both sides of (★) are homogeneous of degree 1 in the \(a_{ij}\): if we replace every \(a_{ij}\) by \(t\,a_{ij}\) then each \(R_i\) and likewise the right–hand side is multiplied by \(t\).  Hence we may without loss of generality impose the normalization

 \(\displaystyle 
\sum_{j=1}^n C_j \;=\;1.
\)

Under this constraint the right–hand side of (★) becomes \(1\), and (★) is equivalent to the single inequality

\[
\sum_{i=1}^m R_i\;\le\;1,
\]
subject to
\[
\sum_{j=1}^n C_j \;=\;1,
\quad
a_{ij}>0.
\]

We now maximize the function
\[
F(a_{11},\dots,a_{mn})
\;:=\;
\sum_{i=1}^m R_i
\;=\;
\sum_{i=1}^m
\Bigl(\sum_{j=1}^n\tfrac1{a_{ij}}\Bigr)^{-1}
\]
subject to the single constraint
\[
G(a_{11},\dots,a_{mn}) 
\;:=\;
\sum_{j=1}^n 
\Bigl(\sum_{i=1}^m a_{ij}\Bigr)^{-1}
\;=\;1.
\]
By the method of Lagrange multipliers there exists \(\lambda\in\Bbb R\) so that at a (positive) extremum \((a_{ij})\) one has for every \(i,j\)
\[
\frac{\partial}{\partial a_{ij}}\Bigl[F(a)-\lambda\,(G(a)-1)\Bigr]
\;=\;
0.
\]
We compute separately
\[
R_i \;=\;\bigl(\sum_{k=1}^n\tfrac1{a_{ik}}\bigr)^{-1},
\]
so
\[
\frac{\partial R_i}{\partial a_{ij}}
\;=\;
\frac{d}{da_{ij}}
\frac1{\sum_{k}1/a_{ik}}
\;=\;
-\,
\frac{1}{\bigl(\sum_{k}1/a_{ik}\bigr)^2}
\;\cdot\;
\bigl(-\tfrac1{a_{ij}^2}\bigr)
\;=\;
\frac{1}{\,a_{ij}^2\,
\bigl(\sum_{k}1/a_{ik}\bigr)^2\,}\,.
\]
Hence
\[
\frac{\partial F}{\partial a_{ij}}
\;=\;
\frac{\partial}{\partial a_{ij}}
\sum_{r=1}^m R_r
\;=\;
\frac{1}{\,a_{ij}^2\,
\bigl(\sum_{k}1/a_{ik}\bigr)^2\,}
\quad
\text{(the term \(r=i\) only).}
\]
Next
\[
G(a)
\;=\;
\sum_{j=1}^n 
\frac1{c_j}
\quad\text{with}\quad
c_j=\sum_{i=1}^m a_{ij},
\]
so
\[
\frac{\partial}{\partial a_{ij}}\,
\frac1{c_j}
\;=\;
-\frac1{c_j^2}\,
\frac{\partial c_j}{\partial a_{ij}}
\;=\;
-\frac1{c_j^2}.
\]
Thus
\[
\frac{\partial G}{\partial a_{ij}}
\;=\;
-\frac1{c_j^2},
\]
and the Lagrange–condition
\(\partial_{a_{ij}}\bigl[F-\lambda(G-1)\bigr]=0\) reads
\[
\frac1{a_{ij}^2\,
\bigl(\sum_{k}1/a_{ik}\bigr)^2}
\;-\;
\lambda\,
\Bigl(-\frac1{c_j^2}\Bigr)
\;=\;
0
\;\;\Longrightarrow\;\;
\frac1{a_{ij}^2\,
\bigl(\sum_{k}1/a_{ik}\bigr)^2}
\;=\;
\frac{\lambda}{\,c_j^2}\,.
\]
Hence for each fixed \(j\),
\[
a_{ij}^2\,
\Bigl(\sum_{k=1}^n\frac1{a_{ik}}\Bigr)^2
\;=\;
\frac{1}{\lambda}\,c_j^2
\quad\text{is independent of }i.
\]
In particular, there exist positive constants \(K_j\) so that
\[
a_{ij}\;\sum_{k=1}^n\frac1{a_{ik}}
\;=\;
K_j,
\qquad
\forall i=1,\dots,m.
\]
But then for any two rows \(i,i'\) and any column \(j\),
\[
\frac{a_{ij}}{a_{i'j}}
\;=\;
\frac{\displaystyle
K_j\big/\sum_k1/a_{ik}
}
{\displaystyle
K_j\big/\sum_k1/a_{i'k}
}
\;=\;
\frac{\sum_{k=1}^n\frac1{a_{i'k}}}
{\sum_{k=1}^n\frac1{a_{ik}}}
\;=\;
\frac{R_{i'}}{R_i},
\]
a ratio independent of \(j\).  Hence there are positive numbers \(x_i,R_i\) and \(y_j\) so that
\[
a_{ij}
\;=\;
\frac{K_j}{\sum_{k}1/a_{ik}}
\;=\;
x_i\,y_j.
\]
That is, the only interior critical points under the constraint \(\sum_jC_j=1\) are matrices of rank one of the form \(a_{ij}=x_i\,y_j\).  On the boundary of the domain (some \(a_{ij}\to0\) or \(\infty\)) at least one row–harmonic mean \(R_i\to0\), hence \(\sum_iR_i\to0\).  But for a rank–one interior solution \(a_{ij}=x_i\,y_j\) one checks directly
\[
\sum_{i=1}^m R_i
\;=\;
\sum_{i=1}^m
\Bigl(\sum_{j=1}^n\frac1{x_i\,y_j}\Bigr)^{-1}
\;=\;
\sum_{i=1}^m
\frac{x_i}{\sum_j1/y_j}
\;=\;
\frac{\sum_i x_i}{\sum_j1/y_j}
\;=\;
1
\]
precisely when \(\sum_jC_j=1\).  Thus the global maximum of \(\sum_iR_i\) under the constraint \(\sum_jC_j=1\) is \(1\).  In the original (un‐normalized) variables this proves

\[
\sum_{i=1}^{m}
\Bigl(\sum_{j=1}^{n}\frac1{a_{ij}}\Bigr)^{-1}
\;\le\;
\Bigl(\sum_{j=1}^{n}
\Bigl(\sum_{i=1}^{m}a_{ij}\Bigr)^{-1}\Bigr)^{-1}.
\]

Equality holds exactly when the matrix \(\{a_{ij}\}\) has rank one, i.e. when
\[
a_{ij}=x_i\,y_j
\]
for some positive vectors \(x=(x_1,\dots,x_m)\) and \(y=(y_1,\dots,y_n)\).  ■
